output:
**Analysis**

We have all permutations of the three letters `a`, `b`, `c`.  We want to know whether, by doing at most one swap of two positions (or doing nothing), we can turn a given permutation into the target string `"abc"`.

Key observations:

1. If the string is already `"abc"`, we need zero swaps → answer is YES.
2. If exactly two positions are “mismatched” with `"abc"`, those two letters must simply be swapped to fix both differences → answer is YES.
3. If all three positions are mismatched, it means the permutation is a 3-cycle (either `"bca"` or `"cab"`), and one swap cannot fix all three → answer is NO.

Hence it suffices to count how many positions differ from `"abc"`.  If that count is ≤ 2, the answer is YES; otherwise NO.

**Implementation in C++**

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int t;
    cin >> t;
    const string target = "abc";

    while (t--) {
        string s;
        cin >> s;
        int mismatches = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            if (s[i] != target[i]) 
                mismatches++;
        }
        // If we have at most 2 mismatches, we can fix them with at most one swap
        cout << (mismatches <= 2 ? "YES\n" : "NO\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

**Explanation of Steps**

1. Read the number of test cases `t`.
2. For each test case:
   - Read the 3-character string `s`.
   - Compare each character of `s` with `"abc"` and count how many positions differ.
   - If the count of mismatches is 0 or 2, print **YES** (0 swaps or 1 swap suffices).
   - Otherwise (3 mismatches), print **NO**.

Here’s the high‐level approach:

1. On a single EC2 instance you attach one ENI (elastic network interface) with 10 secondary private IPv4s.  
2. You allocate 10 Elastic IPs and associate each EIP with one of those private IPs on the ENI.  
3. Your scraper box now “owns” 10 public IPs.  When you launch your ten Python processes you bind each process to one of the instance’s private IPs (so that the SNAT picks the matching EIP as its source).

Below is a minimal Terraform example that does exactly that (you’ll of course need to fill in your own VPC/subnet/security‐group/AMI details):

```hcl
variable "aws_region"  { default = "us-west-2" }
variable "subnet_id"    { default = "subnet-0123456789abcdef0" }
variable "ami"          { default = "ami-0abcdef1234567890" }
variable "instance_type" { default = "t3.small" }
variable "security_group_ids" { type = list(string) }

provider "aws" {
  region = var.aws_region
}

# 1) Create a network interface with 10 private IPs
resource "aws_network_interface" "scraper_eni" {
  subnet_id       = var.subnet_id
  security_groups = var.security_group_ids

  # define 10 secondary IPs in addition to the automatically allocated primary IP
  private_ips = [
    for i in range(10) :
    cidrhost(aws_subnet.my_subnet.cidr_block, 100 + i)
  ]
}

# 2) Allocate 10 Elastic IPs and associate each one to one of the private IPs
resource "aws_eip" "scraper_eips" {
  count = 10
  vpc   = true

  network_interface = aws_network_interface.scraper_eni.id
  private_ip        = aws_network_interface.scraper_eni.private_ips[count.index]
}

# 3) Launch the EC2 instance, attaching that ENI
resource "aws_instance" "scraper" {
  ami           = var.ami
  instance_type = var.instance_type

  # we attach our pre‐built ENI as device_index=0
  network_interface {
    network_interface_id = aws_network_interface.scraper_eni.id
    device_index         = 0
  }

  # … any other userdata, tags, IAM roles, etc …
}

output "public_ips" {
  value = aws_eip.scraper_eips[*].public_ip
}
output "private_ips" {
  value = aws_network_interface.scraper_eni.private_ips
}
```

When you terraform apply this:

• You get one EC2 instance with a single ENI that holds 10 private IPs.  
• Each of those private IPs has an Elastic IP (public IP) stuck on it.  
• Terraform outputs you both the public_ips and the private_ips lists.

On the EC2 instance itself you’ll see your 10 addresses on eth0 (or whatever your primary interface is).  Now you can launch 10 Python/Playwright processes and in each process bind your outgoing socket to one of the private IPs.  For example in Python stdlib sockets you can do:

```python
import socket
sock = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
sock.bind(('10.0.1.100', 0))    # pick one of your secondary private IPs
# …then pass sock to your HTTP client or use SO_BINDTODEVICE, etc …
```

Or if you’re using a higher‐level library that accepts a source_address parameter (e.g. with requests you can do `session.get(url, source_address=('10.0.1.101', 0))`).  Each process you start, bind it to `private_ips[i]` from Terraform’s output and you’ll see its outbound traffic egress on the corresponding Elastic IP.

That way you can run 10 scrapers in parallel on one EC2 host, each with its own public source IP, all managed by Terraform.

Here’s a minimal recipe for putting an AppCompat Toolbar into a Fragment:

1) In your Fragment’s layout (e.g. res/layout/fragment_my.xml) put a Toolbar:

   ```xml
   <?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
   <androidx.coordinatorlayout.widget.CoordinatorLayout
       xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
       xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
       android:layout_width="match_parent"
       android:layout_height="match_parent">

     <com.google.android.material.appbar.AppBarLayout
         android:layout_width="match_parent"
         android:layout_height="wrap_content">

       <com.google.android.material.appbar.MaterialToolbar
           android:id="@+id/toolbar"
           android:layout_width="match_parent"
           android:layout_height="?attr/actionBarSize"
           app:title="My Fragment"
           app:menu="@menu/fragment_menu" />

     </com.google.android.material.appbar.AppBarLayout>

     <!-- rest of your UI below -->
     <FrameLayout
       android:layout_width="match_parent"
       android:layout_height="match_parent"
       app:layout_behavior="@string/appbar_scrolling_view_behavior">
       …
     </FrameLayout>

   </androidx.coordinatorlayout.widget.CoordinatorLayout>
   ```

2) In your Fragment class:

   ```java
   public class MyFragment extends Fragment {
     @Override
     public View onCreateView(LayoutInflater inflater, ViewGroup container,
                              Bundle savedInstanceState) {
       View view = inflater.inflate(R.layout.fragment_my, container, false);

       // 1) Find the toolbar in the fragment’s view
       Toolbar toolbar = view.findViewById(R.id.toolbar);

       // 2) Tell the hosting Activity to use this toolbar as its ActionBar
       AppCompatActivity activity = (AppCompatActivity) getActivity();
       activity.setSupportActionBar(toolbar);

       // 3) If you want Fragment to contribute to the options menu:
       setHasOptionsMenu(true);

       return view;
     }

     @Override
     public void onCreateOptionsMenu(Menu menu, MenuInflater inflater) {
       // inflate your fragment-specific menu here
       // e.g. inflater.inflate(R.menu.fragment_menu, menu);
       super.onCreateOptionsMenu(menu, inflater);
     }

     @Override
     public boolean onOptionsItemSelected(MenuItem item) {
       // handle item clicks
       switch (item.getItemId()) {
         case R.id.action_settings:
           // …
           return true;
       }
       return super.onOptionsItemSelected(item);
     }
   }
   ```

3) Make sure your Activity’s theme **doesn’t** already have a native ActionBar. For example you can use:

   ```xml
   <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.MaterialComponents.Light.NoActionBar">
     <!-- your theme customizations -->
   </style>
   ```

4) If you need an “up” arrow or drawer toggle, configure it in the Fragment (or preferably in the Activity):

   ```java
   ActionBar ab = activity.getSupportActionBar();
   ab.setDisplayHomeAsUpEnabled(true);
   ab.setHomeAsUpIndicator(R.drawable.ic_menu);
   ```

That’s it: your fragment’s toolbar now appears in place of the Activity’s default bar, hosts its own menu items, title, navigation icon, etc.

Here’s an iterative, non-recursive DFS that walks any mix of objects/arrays, finds the first object whose `name` property matches, and returns the path (as a list of keys/indexes) used to reach it.  It also uses a WeakSet to avoid infinite loops on cyclic structures.

```javascript
function findObjectByName(root, targetName) {
  if (typeof root !== 'object' || root === null) return null;

  const visited = new WeakSet();
  const stack = [ { node: root, path: [] } ];

  while (stack.length) {
    const { node, path } = stack.pop();

    if (visited.has(node)) continue;
    visited.add(node);

    // Check if this node matches
    if (node && node.name === targetName) {
      return path;   // found: return the path to this object
    }

    // Traverse its children
    if (Array.isArray(node)) {
      node.forEach((item, idx) => {
        if (item && typeof item === 'object') {
          stack.push({ node: item, path: path.concat(idx) });
        }
      });
    } else {
      for (const key of Object.keys(node)) {
        const child = node[key];
        if (child && typeof child === 'object') {
          stack.push({ node: child, path: path.concat(key) });
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // not found
  return null;
}

// Example:
const data = {
  name: "root",
  children: [
    { name: "a", value: 1 },
    {
      name: "b",
      children: [
        { name: "x", value: 2 },
        { name: "target", info: "here it is" }
      ]
    }
  ]
};

const path = findObjectByName(data, "target");
console.log(path);    // [ "children", 1, "children", 1 ]
// You can use it to walk down from `data` to the found node:
if (path) {
  let node = data;
  for (const step of path) node = node[step];
  console.log(node);  // { name: "target", info: "here it is" }
}
```

instruction:
If I have a TypeScript class:

class Foo {
  ReactProperties: {
    a: string;
  }
}

How do I extract the type of the ReactProperties member object from the type Class?

output:
Here are a couple of ways to pull the `{ a: string }`‐type out of your `Foo` class:

1) If you already have the *instance* type `Foo`, you can use an indexed access type:

```ts
class Foo {
  ReactProperties = {
    a: "hello"
  }
}

// this is exactly { a: string }
type Props1 = Foo["ReactProperties"];
```

2) If you only have the *constructor* (`typeof Foo`), first get the instance with `InstanceType<…>`:

```ts
class Foo {
  ReactProperties = {
    a: "hello",
  }
}

// typeof Foo is the constructor
type Props2 = InstanceType<typeof Foo>["ReactProperties"];
```

3) If you want a reusable generic:

```ts
// For an instance type:
type GetRP<T extends { ReactProperties: any }> = T["ReactProperties"];

// For a constructor type:
type GetRPFromCtor<C extends new (...args: any) => any> =
  InstanceType<C>["ReactProperties"];

// usage
type P1 = GetRP<Foo>;            // { a: string }
type P2 = GetRPFromCtor<typeof Foo>; // { a: string }
```

4) Or with a conditional‐type/`infer`:

```ts
type ExtractRP<T> = T extends { ReactProperties: infer R } ? R : never;

// usage
type P3 = ExtractRP<Foo>;  // { a: string }
```

Any of those will give you the exact type of your `ReactProperties` member.

Below is a simple C solution that runs in O(N) per test case by checking all suffix‐sums of +1/−1 (for ‘1’/‘0’) never exceed 1 in absolute value.  If at any point |suffix_sum|>1 we can answer NO immediately, otherwise YES.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(){
    int T;
    scanf("%d",&T);
    while(T--){
        int N;
        static char S[100005];
        scanf("%d%s",&N,S);
        int suffix = 0, ok = 1;
        for(int i = N-1; i >= 0; i--){
            suffix += (S[i]=='1' ? 1 : -1);
            if(suffix > 1 || suffix < -1){
                ok = 0;
                break;
            }
        }
        puts(ok ? "YES" : "NO");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Explanation of correctness:

- Let w_i = +1 if S[i]=='1' (Alice) and w_i = −1 if S[i]=='0' (Bob).  
- We need  
    max over all non‐decreasing 0≤v₁≤…≤v_N (scaled so v_N=1) of |∑₁ⁿ wᵢ·vᵢ| ≤ 1.  
- By linear programming, the worst v is a 0→1 “step” (i.e. an indicator of a suffix), so the maximum absolute value is  
    maxₖ |∑_{i=k+1..N} wᵢ|.  
- Hence we only need to check that every suffix‐sum of w stays within [−1,1].

Here is a simple C solution.  Observe that you can pair up to (2N–X) rated players each with an unrated player.  Any remaining rated players (X–(2N–X)=2X–2N of them) must be paired among themselves, forming (X–N) matches and thus contributing 2·(X–N) to the count of rated‐vs‐rated players.  Hence the answer is:

    answer = max(0, 2·(X–N))

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int T;
    if (scanf("%d", &T) != 1) return 0;
    while (T--) {
        int N, X;
        scanf("%d %d", &N, &X);
        int ans = 0;
        if (X > N) {
            ans = 2 * (X - N);
        }
        printf("%d\n", ans);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Explanation of the key step:
- You have X rated and (2N–X) unrated.
- You can “use up” at most one unrated per rated player, so you can avoid rated vs. rated for up to min(X, 2N–X) rated players.
- If X≤N then 2N–X≥X, so you pair every rated with an unrated → answer=0.
- If X>N, you pair 2N–X of the rated with all the unrated, leaving X–(2N–X)=2X–2N rated unpaired with unrated.  These must fight among themselves → (2X–2N)/2=(X–N) rated-vs-rated matches, each contributing 2 rated players → answer=2(X–N).

Onnx Runtime on Android will by default only spin up a very small number of threads on the CPU execution provider, so you will rarely ever see anywhere near 100% of your big-cores being used unless you explicitly tell it to. Your two easiest paths are:

 1. Turn up OnnxRuntime’s own thread-pool (intra‐op / inter‐op)  
 2. Switch to or append the XNNPACK EP and tell it how many threads you want

——————————————————————————————————  
1) Tuning the CPU EP’s thread‐pool  
---------------------------------  
If you’re using the C++/C API you do something like:  
```cpp
  Ort::SessionOptions so;
  // run each node in parallel on N threads
  so.SetIntraOpNumThreads(N);  
  // run independent subgraphs in parallel on M threads
  so.SetInterOpNumThreads(M);  
  // your normal session creation…
  Ort::Session session(env, modelPath.c_str(), so);
```

If you’re using the Java/Kotlin bindings it looks like:  
```java
  OrtEnvironment env = OrtEnvironment.getEnvironment();
  SessionOptions so = new SessionOptions();
  // spin up N threads per node
  so.setIntraOpNumThreads(N);
  // spin up M threads across nodes
  so.setInterOpNumThreads(M);
  OrtSession session = env.createSession(modelBytes, so);
```

A good starting point is to set both to Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors() (or to the number of “big” cores on your phone, e.g. 4 or 6).  You’ll almost certainly get more CPU utilization by cranking these up.

If you actually built ONNX Runtime with OpenMP support you can also set the usual environment variables (OMP_NUM_THREADS, KMP_AFFINITY, etc.) but on most stock mobile builds OpenMP is disabled and ORT’s own threadpool is in use.

—————————————————————————————————  
2) Using the XNNPACK EP  
------------------------  
XNNPACK is a highly optimized mobile‐first engine that actually benefits from multithreading more aggressively.  You can append it like so:

C++  
```cpp
  Ort::SessionOptions so;
  // spawn 4 XNNPACK threads (try tuning this)
  OrtStatus* status = OrtSessionOptionsAppendExecutionProvider_XNNPACK(so, /*threadPoolSize=*/4);
  // … then create your session as normal
```

Java  
```java
  SessionOptions so = new SessionOptions();
  // tell XNNPACK: use 4 threads
  so.addXNNPACK(4);
  OrtSession session = env.createSession(modelBytes, so);
```

Again, experiment with threadPoolSize = number of cores (or big-cores) on your test device.

—————————————————————————————————  
Other little gotchas  
---------------------  
• Make sure your CPU governor isn’t locked into a “power save” mode on your phone.  Some OEMs under-clock the big cores.  
• If your individual ONNX graph is very shallow or each operator is extremely cheap, you will hit scheduling overhead before you ever get 100% usage.  In that case you may actually want to batch inputs or switch to a mixed GPU/NNAPI approach.  
• Profile with Android Studio’s System Trace or perfetto to verify you’re actually keeping all cores busy.  
• If you really need more throughput consider using NNAPI or the GPU EP—those can sometimes yield better scaling on mobile SoCs.

In short: bump up SetIntraOpNumThreads / setNumThreads to match your core count, or switch on XNNPACK with a larger thread pool.  You should see CPU utilization climb from ~40% toward 90–100% once your graph is large enough to keep all threads busy.

