Hello, what do you think of this arduino code in regards to understandability, optimization and size?
Any suggestions for improvements?

void cycleLEDs(int interval) {
  const int nOutPins = sizeof(outPins) / sizeof(outPins[0]);
  static unsigned long lastChange = 0;
  static int currIndex = 0; // Use static to retain value between function calls
  int nextIndex = 0;

  if (millis() >= lastChange + abs(interval)) {

    // Determine direction
    int direction = interval < 0 ? -1 : 1;

    // Update secondary index
    nextIndex = currIndex + direction;
    if (nextIndex < 0) {
      nextIndex = nOutPins - 1;
    } else if (nextIndex >= nOutPins) {
      nextIndex = 0;
    }

    // Turn off all LEDs
    for (int i = 0; i < nOutPins; i++) {
      if (i == currIndex || i == nextIndex){
        digitalWrite(outPins[i], HIGH);
      } else {
        digitalWrite(outPins[i], LOW);
      }      
    }

    // Update current index
    currIndex += direction;
    if (currIndex < 0) {
      currIndex = nOutPins - 1;
    } else if (currIndex >= nOutPins) {
      currIndex = 0;
    }

    // Update timer
    lastChange = millis();
  }
}

Thank you for your help, i value your input.

output:
Here are a few suggestions, from “small‐tweaks” for readability all the way up to a more compact refactoring.  Pick and choose what makes sense for your project:

1.  Avoid recomputing array length on every call  
    If `outPins` is global or file-scope you can do e.g.  
    ```cpp
    const byte outPins[] = { 2,3,4,5,6 };
    const byte N = sizeof(outPins)/sizeof(outPins[0]);
    ```  
    That way you don’t need `const int nOutPins = …` every time.

2.  Use subtraction on `millis()` to handle rollover safely  
    Rather than  
    ```cpp
    if (millis() >= lastChange + abs(interval))
    ```  
    it’s generally safer to write  
    ```cpp
    unsigned long now = millis();
    if (now - lastChange >= (unsigned long)abs(interval)) {
      lastChange = now;
      …
    }
    ```  
    This will behave correctly even if `millis()` wraps.

3.  Simplify index-wrapping with modulo arithmetic  
    Instead of manually checking bounds, you can write
    ```cpp
    // +N to keep it non­negative before the %:
    currIndex = (currIndex + dir + N) % N;
    ```
    and likewise compute `nextIndex` the same way.

4.  Update only the LEDs that actually change  
    Right now you’re doing a full `for`-loop that writes every pin HIGH/LOW.  
    If you only ever turn two LEDs on then off, you could remember the “old” pair,
    switch them off, compute the new pair, and switch them on.  That saves `N−4`
    calls to `digitalWrite()` at each transition.

5.  Give your variables descriptive names  
    For example:
      • `interval` → `stepDelayMs`  
      • `currIndex` → `activeLed`  
      • `direction` → `step`  

— — — — — —   

Below is a compact rewrite incorporating item 2, 3 and more descriptive names.  It still does a full loop for clarity, but is only about half the lines:

```cpp
// top of file
const byte outPins[] = { 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 };
const byte LED_COUNT = sizeof outPins / sizeof *outPins;

void cycleLEDs(int stepDelayMs)
{
  static unsigned long lastChange = 0;
  static int  activeLed   = 0;
  int          step        = (stepDelayMs < 0 ? -1 : +1);
  unsigned long now        = millis();
  unsigned long delayMs    = (unsigned long)abs(stepDelayMs);

  if (now - lastChange < delayMs) return;    // not time yet
  lastChange = now;

  // compute the new LED index
  int nextLed = (activeLed + step + LED_COUNT) % LED_COUNT;

  // update all LEDs in one loop
  for (int i = 0; i < LED_COUNT; i++) {
    bool on = (i == activeLed || i == nextLed);
    digitalWrite(outPins[i], on ? HIGH : LOW);
  }

  // advance for the next pass
  activeLed = nextLed;
}
```

Why this is (arguably) easier to read and slightly smaller:

  * We do the timing check in a single `if` with `now - lastChange`.
  * We compute `step` only once instead of in each branch.
  * We use `(x + N) % N` to wrap indices in one line.
  * We’ve renamed variables for clarity.
  * We moved the array-size calculation out of the function.

If you want to squeeze out even more performance/size:

  - Track just the two pins that need changing and call `digitalWrite()` only on them.
  - Cache `abs(stepDelayMs)` in a `static const unsigned long period;` if you never change it.
  - Place `outPins[]` in PROGMEM if you’re very tight on RAM.

Hope that helps!

