Please rewrite the following pseudo C code as an equivalent code that is easier to read (assume that every variable works, even if it's not defined):

```
handleCmdLineArgs(char *param_1)
{
  uint uVar2;
  uint uVar3;
  int iVar4;
  char *pcVar5;
  char cVar1;

  uVar3 = 0xffffffff;
  uVar2 = 0;
  iVar4 = 0;
  pcVar5 = param_1;
  do {
    if (uVar3 == 0) break;
    uVar3 = uVar3 - 1;
    cVar1 = *pcVar5;
    pcVar5 = pcVar5 + 1;
  } while (cVar1 != '\0');
  if (0 < (int)(~uVar3 - 2)) {
    do {
      uVar2 = RK_CheckSJIS(uVar2 & 0xffffff00 | (uint)(byte)param_1[iVar4]);
      if (uVar2 == 1) {
        iVar4 = iVar4 + 1;
      }
      else if (param_1[iVar4] == '/') {
        uVar2 = uVar2 & 0xffffff00 | (uint)(byte)param_1[iVar4 + 1] | 0x20;
        if ((char)uVar2 == 'w') {
          IsWindowedMode = 1;
        }
        else if ((char)uVar2 == 'f') {
          IsWindowedMode = 0;
        }
      }
      iVar4 = iVar4 + 1;
    } while (iVar4 < (int)(~uVar3 - 2));
  }
}
```

output:
Here’s a more idiomatic, commented C‐style rewrite.  It does exactly what your pseudo code does, but in a much more readable form:

```c
#include <string.h>
#include <ctype.h>   // for tolower()

// externals we assume exist somewhere:
extern int RK_CheckSJIS(unsigned int byteAndState);
extern int IsWindowedMode;

void handleCmdLineArgs(const char *cmdLine)
{
    // 1) compute the length of the argument string
    size_t len = strlen(cmdLine);

    // if there are fewer than 3 chars, there cannot be any “/w” or “/f” options
    if (len <= 2) {
        return;
    }

    // sjisState holds any high‐order bits RK_CheckSJIS wants to preserve
    unsigned int sjisState = 0;

    // Scan from i = 0 to i < len-1, so we always have room to look at cmdLine[i+1]
    for (size_t i = 0; i < len - 1; )
    {
        unsigned char c = (unsigned char)cmdLine[i];

        // Feed the current byte (plus any saved state) into RK_CheckSJIS
        sjisState = RK_CheckSJIS((sjisState & 0xFF00) | c);

        if (sjisState == 1) {
            // We’re in a multi‐byte SJIS sequence: skip the next byte too
            i += 2;
            continue;
        }

        // If this is a slash, look at the next character for 'w' or 'f'
        if (c == '/' && i + 1 < len) {
            char opt = tolower((unsigned char)cmdLine[i + 1]);

            if (opt == 'w') {
                IsWindowedMode = 1;
            }
            else if (opt == 'f') {
                IsWindowedMode = 0;
            }

            // consume both '/' and its option-letter
            i += 2;
        }
        else {
            // normal single‐byte character, just advance one
            i += 1;
        }
    }
}
```

Explanation of the main points:

• We first do a straightforward `strlen`.  
• If the total length is ≤ 2, there’s no room for a “/x” switch, so we return immediately.  
• We walk the string with an index `i`, always making sure there’s at least one more character after `i` so that `/` + letter don’t overrun.  
• Before testing for `/`, we call `RK_CheckSJIS(...)`. If it returns `1`, that tells us we just saw an SJIS lead byte and must skip the next byte as part of the same character.  
• Otherwise, if the current character is `/`, we look at `cmdLine[i+1]`, downcase it, and if it is `w` or `f` we flip `IsWindowedMode` accordingly.  
• We then advance `i` by either 1, 2, or 2 again (in the SJIS‐skip case), exactly matching the control flow of your original pseudo‐code.

Here’s a minimal example that uses Flexbox to pin one button to the left and the other to the right in the same row:

HTML  
```html
<div class="button‐row">
  <button class="btn left">Left Button</button>
  <button class="btn right">Right Button</button>
</div>
```

CSS  
```css
.button‐row {
  display: flex;
  /* space-between will push first child to very left and last child to very right */
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;    /* vertically center if buttons have different heights */
  padding: 0.5em;         /* optional spacing around */
  width: 100%;            /* stretch to full available width */
  box-sizing: border-box; /* include padding in width */
}

/* optional button styling */
.btn {
  padding: 0.5em 1em;
  font-size: 1rem;
  cursor: pointer;
}

/* if you ever need more control, you can also do this: */
/* .right { margin-left: auto; } */
```

Explanation of the key bits:

1. `.button‐row { display: flex; }` turns the container into a flexbox.  
2. `justify-content: space-between;` pushes the first flex item all the way to the left and the last flex item all the way to the right, with any extra space split between them.  
3. If you only have those two buttons, that’s all you need. If you ever need more complex layouts (e.g. a logo on the left, a nav in the center, and your buttons on the right), Flexbox will naturally adapt.

Alternative: if you only want to affect the right button, you could leave `justify-content` alone and do:  
```css
.right {
  margin-left: auto;
}
```  
That tells the right‐hand button to consume all available space to its left, effectively pushing itself to the right edge.

Here are three simple JavaScript examples for approximating π. You can pick whichever suits your needs (speed vs. simplicity vs. ease of understanding).

1. Leibniz (Gregory) series  
   – Very simple, but converges slowly (need millions of terms for a few decimals).

    function leibnizPi(iterations) {
      let sum = 0;
      let sign = 1;
      for (let i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
        sum += sign / (2 * i + 1);
        sign = -sign;
      }
      return sum * 4;
    }

    // Example usage:
    console.log(leibnizPi(1e6)); // around 3.1415916536

2. Monte Carlo simulation  
   – Uses random sampling; good for demonstrating statistical methods but not super-efficient.

    function monteCarloPi(numPoints) {
      let inside = 0;
      for (let i = 0; i < numPoints; i++) {
        const x = Math.random();
        const y = Math.random();
        if (x * x + y * y <= 1) inside++;
      }
      return (inside / numPoints) * 4;
    }

    // Example usage:
    console.log(monteCarloPi(1e7)); // improves as numPoints grows

3. Gauss–Legendre algorithm  
   – Converges quadratically; you get many correct digits with few iterations.

    function gaussLegendrePi(iterations) {
      let a = 1;
      let b = 1 / Math.sqrt(2);
      let t = 1 / 4;
      let p = 1;
      for (let i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
        const aNext = (a + b) / 2;
        const bNext = Math.sqrt(a * b);
        const tNext = t - p * Math.pow(a - aNext, 2);
        a = aNext;
        b = bNext;
        t = tNext;
        p = 2 * p;
      }
      return Math.pow(a + b, 2) / (4 * t);
    }

    // Example usage:
    console.log(gaussLegendrePi(5)); // should already give ~3.141592653589793

Pick the method that best fits your accuracy/performance trade-off.

Below is a simple C# console-app that:

 1. Takes Math.PI, rounds it to 5 decimal places  
 2. Converts that to an integer (× 100 000)  
 3. XORs that integer with a “key”  
 4. XORs the result with the same key again (which recovers the original)  
 5. Prints out each step  

```csharp
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // 1) Compute π and round to 5 decimals
        double pi = Math.PI;
        double roundedPi = Math.Round(pi, 5);
        Console.WriteLine("Pi to 5 decimals: " + roundedPi);

        // 2) Convert to integer by shifting decimal point
        //    (3.14159 * 100000) => 314159
        int piInt = (int)(roundedPi * 100_000);
        Console.WriteLine("As integer (π*100000): " + piInt);

        // 3) Define an XOR key
        int key = 0x5A5A5;  // you can pick any int value here

        // 4) XOR once
        int encrypted = piInt ^ key;
        Console.WriteLine("After first XOR (encrypted): " + encrypted);

        // 5) XOR again with the same key to decrypt
        int decrypted = encrypted ^ key;
        Console.WriteLine("After second XOR (decrypted int): " + decrypted);

        // 6) Convert back to floating-point decimal
        double recoveredPi = decrypted / 100_000.0;
        Console.WriteLine("Recovered Pi: " + recoveredPi);
    }
}
```

Explanation of the key points:

• Math.Round(pi, 5) gives you 3.14159.  
• Multiplying by 100 000 and casting to int turns it into 314159.  
• Bitwise XOR with a constant “key” scrambles it:  
    encrypted = 314159 ^ key  
• XORing that encrypted value with the same key returns the original:  
    decrypted = encrypted ^ key == 314159  
• Finally we divide by 100 000.0 to get back 3.14159.

Here’s a minimal “chat‐app” in pure HTML + vanilla JS using Transformers.js and the distilled BlenderBot model. Just drop this in an `index.html`, run it over `httpS` (or a local dev server), and you’ll get a two‐way chat.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8"/>
  <title>BlenderBot-400M-Distill Chat</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: sans-serif; margin: 20px; }
    #chat { max-width: 600px; margin-bottom: 10px; }
    .msg { padding: 6px; border-radius: 4px; margin-bottom: 6px; }
    .user { background: #d1e7ff; text-align: right; }
    .bot  { background: #f1f0f0; }
    #controls { display: flex; max-width: 600px; }
    #input { flex: 1; padding: 8px; font-size: 1em; }
    #send  { padding: 8px 16px; }
  </style>
  <!-- get transformers.js from unpkg -->
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/@xenova/transformers@2.3.0/dist/transformers.min.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>BlenderBot-400M-Distill Chat</h1>
  <div id="chat"></div>
  <div id="controls">
    <input id="input" type="text" placeholder="Say something..." />
    <button id="send">Send</button>
  </div>

  <script>
  (async ()=> {
    // 1. Create a conversational pipeline
    const pipeline = await window.transformers.pipeline(
      "conversational",
      "Xenova/facebook/blenderbot-400m-distill"  // or "facebook/blenderbot-400m-distill"
    );

    // 2. Keep a Conversation object to hold history
    let conv = null;

    // 3. Helper to append messages
    function append(role, text) {
      const d = document.createElement("div");
      d.classList.add("msg", role);
      d.textContent = text;
      document.getElementById("chat").appendChild(d);
      // scroll to bottom
      d.scrollIntoView({ behavior: "smooth" });
    }

    // 4. Wire up the send button
    document.getElementById("send").onclick = async () => {
      const inputEl = document.getElementById("input");
      const userText = inputEl.value.trim();
      if (!userText) return;
      inputEl.value = "";

      // show user message
      append("user", userText);

      // first time: new Conversation, thereafter reuse to preserve context
      if (!conv) {
        conv = new window.transformers.Conversation(userText);
      } else {
        conv.add_user_input(userText);
      }

      // 5. Run the pipeline
      await pipeline(conv);

      // 6. Get latest bot reply
      const botReply = conv.generated_responses.slice(-1)[0];
      append("bot", botReply);
    };
  })();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

Explanation of the key parts:

1. Include Transformers.js via a `<script>` tag from a CDN (here unpkg).  
2. Call `transformers.pipeline("conversational", MODEL_ID)` to load the BlenderBot-400M-Distill model and tokenizer in the browser.  
3. Maintain a `Conversation` object across turns so that the model remembers chat history.  
4. On each “Send” click:
   - Append the user’s text to the UI.
   - Add it to your `Conversation` (first turn you initialize it, subsequent turns you call `conv.add_user_input(...)`).
   - Call `await pipeline(conv)`. After it resolves, `conv.generated_responses` holds all replies; grab the last one.  
   - Append the bot’s reply to the UI.  

That’s really all there is to it in vanilla JavaScript. You can of course improve it by:  
• Disabling the Send button while loading  
• Handling errors/timeouts  
• Styling the chat bubbles  
• Hosting over HTTPS (model files are pulled from Hugging Face)  
• Deploying behind a light Node or Python static server if needed.

Here’s a self-contained example of how you can build a “radial” or “circular” slider using plain SVG + JavaScript. The idea is:

 1. Draw a stack of concentric circles as your “ticks.”  
 2. Draw a draggable handle (a small circle) on the circumference of the currently selected radius.  
 3. When the user drags (or clicks) anywhere in the SVG, compute the distance from that point to the center → that’s your new radius → clamp it between your min/max → update the handle position and your value display.

----  
Example (copy/paste into an HTML file):

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Circular Radius Slider</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: sans-serif; }
    #slider { border: 1px solid #ddd; touch-action: none; }
    .tick { stroke: #ccc; fill: none; }
    #handle { fill: steelblue; cursor: pointer; }
    #value { margin-top: .5em; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<h4>Circular Radius Slider</h4>
<svg id="slider" width="300" height="300"></svg>
<div id="value">Radius: <span id="rad">0</span>px</div>

<script>
(function(){
  const svg    = document.getElementById('slider');
  const W      = svg.clientWidth;
  const H      = svg.clientHeight;
  const cx     = W/2, cy = H/2;

  const minR   = 10;    // minimum radius
  const maxR   = 120;   // maximum radius
  const step   = 20;    // tick spacing
  let   curR   = 60;    // starting radius

  // Draw ticks
  for(let r = minR; r <= maxR; r += step) {
    let c = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","circle");
    c.setAttribute("cx", cx);
    c.setAttribute("cy", cy);
    c.setAttribute("r", r);
    c.setAttribute("class","tick");
    svg.appendChild(c);
  }

  // Draw handle
  const handle = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","circle");
  handle.setAttribute("id","handle");
  handle.setAttribute("r",8);
  svg.appendChild(handle);

  // Draw a line from center to handle (optional visual)
  const line = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","line");
  line.setAttribute("stroke","#888");
  line.setAttribute("stroke-width",2);
  svg.appendChild(line);

  // Value display
  const valEl = document.getElementById('rad');

  // Compute handle position for a given radius R (fixed angle at 0°)
  function updateVisual(R, angle = 0) {
    // Angle in radians:
    const a = angle * Math.PI/180;
    const hx = cx + R * Math.cos(a);
    const hy = cy - R * Math.sin(a);   // SVG y‐axis goes down
    handle.setAttribute("cx", hx);
    handle.setAttribute("cy", hy);
    line.setAttribute("x1", cx);
    line.setAttribute("y1", cy);
    line.setAttribute("x2", hx);
    line.setAttribute("y2", hy);
    valEl.textContent = Math.round(R);
  }

  // Initialize
  updateVisual(curR);

  // Dragging logic
  let dragging = false;

  function pointerDown(evt) {
    dragging = true;
    pointerMove(evt);
    svg.setPointerCapture(evt.pointerId);
  }

  function pointerUp(evt) {
    dragging = false;
    svg.releasePointerCapture(evt.pointerId);
  }

  function pointerMove(evt) {
    if (!dragging) return;
    const rect = svg.getBoundingClientRect();
    const mx   = evt.clientX - rect.left;
    const my   = evt.clientY - rect.top;
    const dx   = mx - cx, dy = my - cy;
    let   R    = Math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy);
    R = Math.max(minR, Math.min(maxR, R));
    // (optional) snap to step
    // R = Math.round(R/step)*step;
    curR = R;
    // compute angle if you want the handle to follow angular position
    const angle = Math.atan2(cy - my, mx - cx) * 180/Math.PI;
    updateVisual(curR, angle);
  }

  svg.addEventListener('pointerdown', pointerDown);
  svg.addEventListener('pointermove', pointerMove);
  svg.addEventListener('pointerup',   pointerUp);
  svg.addEventListener('pointerleave,pointercancel', pointerUp);

})();
</script>

</body>
</html>

How this works:

 1. We set up an SVG of 300×300 and pick its center (cx,cy).  
 2. We draw concentric circles (“ticks”) every `step` pixels from `minR` to `maxR`.  
 3. We create a draggable `handle` (a small circle) and an optional radial line for clarity.  
 4. On pointer-down we start tracking, on pointer-move we compute the mouse’s distance to the center → clamp → update the handle’s `cx,cy` and the line. We also optionally compute the angle so that the handle really follows your cursor around.  
 5. We display the current radius in a `<div>` below.

You can of course extend this with:

 • Snapping to the nearest tick (uncomment the “snap to step” line)  
 • Touch support (SVG pointer events already cover mouse & touch)  
 • Custom styling, color gradients, easing…  
 • Converting the radius to an actual “value” (e.g. map 10–120px → 0–100)

That’s it—no external libraries required. Feel free to adapt the code to Canvas or WebGL if you prefer, but SVG + pointer events is the simplest path for most web apps.

Here’s a minimal example showing how you might define an abstract base class `Shape` and then subclass it with `Circle`.  You’ll need to have the `abc` module and `math` available.

```python
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod
import math

class Shape(ABC):
    """Abstract base class for all shapes."""

    @abstractmethod
    def area(self):
        """Return the area of the shape."""
        pass

    @abstractmethod
    def perimeter(self):
        """Return the perimeter (or circumference) of the shape."""
        pass

    def __str__(self):
        return f"{self.__class__.__name__}: area={self.area():.2f}, perimeter={self.perimeter():.2f}"


class Circle(Shape):
    """Circle shape defined by its radius."""

    def __init__(self, radius):
        if radius <= 0:
            raise ValueError("Radius must be positive")
        self.radius = radius

    def area(self):
        """Return the area πr²."""
        return math.pi * self.radius ** 2

    def perimeter(self):
        """Return the circumference 2πr."""
        return 2 * math.pi * self.radius


# Example usage
if __name__ == "__main__":
    c = Circle(3)
    print(c)                    # Circle: area=28.27, perimeter=18.85
    print("Area:", c.area())    # Area: 28.274333882308138
    print("Circumference:", c.perimeter())
```

Explanation:

1. Shape is marked as an abstract base class (ABC) with two abstract methods: `area` and `perimeter`.  
