    // ... existing ctor/factories ...

    /**
     * Fetch a one-time snapshot of the last N lines from a Pod's container.
     */
    public String getPodLogs(String namespace,
                             String podName,
                             String containerName,
                             int tailLines) throws IOException, InterruptedException {
        String uri = String.format(
            "%s/api/v1/namespaces/%s/pods/%s/log?container=%s&tailLines=%d",
            apiServerUrl, namespace, podName, containerName, tailLines);

        HttpRequest req = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
            .uri(URI.create(uri))
            .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(20))
            .header("Authorization", "Bearer " + bearerToken)
            .GET()
            .build();

        HttpResponse<String> resp = httpClient.send(req,
            HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());

        if (resp.statusCode() / 100 != 2) {
            throw new IOException("Failed to get logs: HTTP " + resp.statusCode() +
                                  " => " + resp.body());
        }
        return resp.body();
    }

    /**
     * Stream logs (follow=true).  The caller provides a consumer of each log line.
     */
    public void streamPodLogs(String namespace,
                              String podName,
                              String containerName,
                              int tailLines,
                              java.util.function.Consumer<String> onLine) throws Exception {

        String uri = String.format(
            "%s/api/v1/namespaces/%s/pods/%s/log?container=%s&follow=true&tailLines=%d",
            apiServerUrl, namespace, podName, containerName, tailLines);

        HttpRequest req = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
            .uri(URI.create(uri))
            .timeout(Duration.ZERO)  // no timeout on streams
            .header("Authorization", "Bearer " + bearerToken)
            .GET()
            .build();

        httpClient.sendAsync(req, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofLines())
          .thenAccept(resp -> {
              if (resp.statusCode() / 100 != 2) {
                  throw new RuntimeException("Failed to stream logs: HTTP " + resp.statusCode());
              }
              resp.body().subscribe(new Flow.Subscriber<>() {
                  private Flow.Subscription sub;
                  @Override
                  public void onSubscribe(Flow.Subscription subscription) {
                      this.sub = subscription;
                      subscription.request(1);
                  }
                  @Override
                  public void onNext(String line) {
                      onLine.accept(line);
                      sub.request(1);
                  }
                  @Override
                  public void onError(Throwable throwable) {
                      throwable.printStackTrace();
                  }
                  @Override
                  public void onComplete() {
                      System.out.println("Log stream completed");
                  }
              });
          })
          .join();  // block caller until subscription is set up
    }
}
```

5. Usage example  

```java
public class Demo {
  public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
    KubernetesHttpClient client = KubernetesHttpClient.inCluster(); 
    // Or: .fromKubeConfig()

    // 1) One-shot last 100 lines:
    String logBlob = client.getPodLogs("default", "my-pod-1234", "my-container", 100);
    System.out.println("--- last 100 lines ---");
    System.out.println(logBlob);

    // 2) Follow stream in real time:
    client.streamPodLogs("default", "my-pod-1234", "my-container", 10,
      line -> System.out.println("<< " + line));
  }
}
```

Tips & next steps  
• Add query-params (sinceSeconds, timestamps=true, limitBytes…) as you need.  
• Handle reconnect logic on stream timeouts or TCP resets.  
• Expose a fluent builder API (e.g. PodLogRequest.builder()…) for consumers.  
• Consider back-pressure and buffering (if you get huge log volume).  
• In production you may want to support exec (`/exec`) or port-forward; same pattern but with WebSockets.

With this structure you now have a minimal Kubernetes logs client with zero external dependencies (except for optional YAML parsing). From here you can turn it into a full-blown library!

In SvelteKit your “delete-note” action is firing (and the row disappears on the server) but your list is coming from a `load()` and that `load()` never automatically re-runs after a non-redirecting form action. So your browser still sees the old list of notes.

There are three common ways to make the UI pick up the change:

1) return a redirect from your action  
2) manually invalidate the page’s data from the client  
3) update a client store yourself

---

1) RETURN A REDIRECT (classic full reload)

If you don’t care about progressive enhancement you can simply do:

```ts
// +page.server.ts
import { redirect } from '@sveltejs/kit';

export const actions = {
  delete: async ({ request }) => {
    const form = await request.formData();
    const id   = form.get('id');
    await db.deleteNote(id);
    throw redirect(303, '.');    // ← this forces a full page-reload
  }
};
```

When the action returns a redirect SvelteKit will do a normal navigation to the same page, re-running your `load()` and pulling in the fresh note list.

---

2) USE `use:enhance` + `invalidate()` (Ajax + automatic re-load)

If you want to keep the form “Ajaxy” you can use SvelteKit’s built-in `enhance` helper and after the action completes call `invalidate()` or `invalidateAll()` from `$app/navigation` so your `load()` re-runs:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { enhance } from '$app/forms';
  import { invalidate } from '$app/navigation';
  let form: HTMLFormElement;

  const onEnhance = () => {
    return {
      after: async ({ result }) => {
        // if you want you could inspect result.data or result.type here
        await invalidate();        // re-run this page’s load()
      }
    };
  };
</script>

<form use:enhance={onEnhance} bind:this={form} method="post" action="?/delete">
  <button name="id" value="{note.id}">Delete</button>
</form>
```

Now your action will run over fetch, the page won’t do a full reload, but once the form returns you explicitly re-load the note list.

---

3) UPDATE A MANUAL STORE

If you already keep your notes in a writable store you can skip re-running `load()` entirely and just do:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { notes } from '../stores';
  async function onDelete(id: string) {
    await fetch('/notes?/delete', {
      method: 'POST',
      body: new URLSearchParams({ id })
    });
    // locally remove it
    notes.update((arr) => arr.filter((n) => n.id !== id));
  }
</script>

<button on:click={() => onDelete(note.id)}>Delete</button>
```

This is the most manual but the fastest client-side update if you’re already using a store.

---

Summary

• By default a non-redirecting SvelteKit form action does not re-run the page’s `load()`  
• If you want the notes list to refresh you must either:  
  – throw a `redirect(303, '.')` in your server action (full reload)  
  – use `use:enhance` + `invalidate()/invalidateAll()` (fetch + auto reload)  
  – or manage your own store and remove the item client-side after the fetch.

