Here’s a quick step-by-step example of how you can create a middleware that only allows requests coming from a hard-coded list of IPs. Feel free to move the IP list into a config file or your .env later if you wish.

1) Generate a new middleware  
```bash
php artisan make:middleware CheckIp
```

2) Open `app/Http/Middleware/CheckIp.php` and edit it like this:  
```php
<?php

namespace App\Http\Middleware;

use Closure;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

class CheckIp
{
    /**
     * List of IPs allowed to access the application.
     * You can also pull this from config('app.allowed_ips') or env('ALLOWED_IPS')
     */
    protected $allowedIps = [
        '123.45.67.89',
        '::1',          // localhost IPv6
        '127.0.0.1',    // localhost IPv4
        // add more IPs here …
    ];

    /**
     * Handle an incoming request.
     */
    public function handle(Request $request, Closure $next)
    {
        $ip = $request->ip();

        if (! in_array($ip, $this->allowedIps, true)) {
            // Option A: abort with 403
            abort(403, 'Unauthorized.');

            // Option B: custom response
            // return response()->json(['message' => 'Forbidden'], 403);
        }

        return $next($request);
    }
}
```

3) Register the middleware  
In `app/Http/Kernel.php` add it to either the `$middleware` (global) or `$routeMiddleware` array:

```php
// app/Http/Kernel.php

protected $routeMiddleware = [
    // …
    'check.ip' => \App\Http\Middleware\CheckIp::class,
];
```

4) Apply it to routes  
In your routes (web.php or api.php) you can now do:

```php
Route::middleware('check.ip')->group(function(){
    Route::get('/dashboard', 'DashboardController@index');
    // other routes…
});
```

Or on a single route:

```php
Route::get('/admin', 'AdminController@index')->middleware('check.ip');
```

That’s it. Any request coming from an IP not in your `$allowedIps` list will receive a 403 forbidden. 

Optional enhancements:  
• Move the IP list into `config/ip.php` or into an `.env` variable for easier maintenance.  
• If you’re behind a proxy or load-balancer, consider trusting proxy headers so `$request->ip()` returns the real client IP.

