output:
Here’s what this snippet is doing, step by step:

1. Collecting elements  
   • `steps`  
     – `Array.from(document.querySelectorAll("form .step"))`  
     – Grabs every element inside your form that has the class `step` and turns the NodeList into a true array.  
     – Each “step” could be, for example, a fieldset or div that you want to show one at a time.  
   • `nextBtn` and `prevBtn`  
     – `document.querySelectorAll("form .next-btn")` / `.previous-btn`  
     – Grabs all of your “Next” and “Previous” buttons inside the form. These are still NodeLists.  
   • `form`  
     – `document.querySelector("form")`  
     – A reference to the form element itself.

2. Wiring up click listeners on the Next and Previous buttons  
   nextBtn.forEach(button => {  
     button.addEventListener("click", () => changeStep("next"));  
   });  
   prevBtn.forEach(button => {  
     button.addEventListener("click", () => changeStep("prev"));  
   });  
   • For each Next button: when clicked, call `changeStep("next")`.  
   • For each Previous button: when clicked, call `changeStep("prev")`.  

3. Handling form submission  
   form.addEventListener("submit", e => {  
     e.preventDefault();  
     // Gather all inputs  
     const inputs = [];  
     form.querySelectorAll("input").forEach(input => {  
       const { name, value } = input;           // destructuring  
       inputs.push({ name, value });             // push an object {name, value}  
     });  
     console.log(inputs);                        // log collected data  
     form.reset();                               // clear all fields  
   });  
   • `e.preventDefault()` stops the browser from actually submitting / reloading the page.  
   • The code then collects every `<input>`’s `name` and `value` into an array of objects.  
   • Finally it logs that array and resets the form.

4. The changeStep function  
   function changeStep(btn) {  
     let index = 0;  
     const active = document.querySelector(".active");     // the currently visible step  
     index = steps.indexOf(active);                        // find its position in the steps array  
     steps[index].classList.remove("active");              // hide current step  
     if (btn === "next") index++;                          // move index forward  
     else if (btn === "prev") index--;                     // or backward  
     steps[index].classList.add("active");                 // show the new step  
   }  
   • `.active` is a CSS class you’ve presumably defined to make one step visible.  
   • When you click Next or Previous, it finds which step is active, removes that class, adjusts the index, and adds `.active` to the new step.

Putting it all together:  
– You have a multi-step form where only one “step” container is visible at a time.  
– Clicking “Next”/“Previous” just swaps which `.step` has the `.active` class.  
– Submitting the form collects all inputs, logs them, and resets everything.

