The warning you’re seeing is IntelliJ (or FindBugs/SpotBugs) telling you that doing

  modCount++;

on a volatile int is not actually an atomic operation.  The compiler will generate something like:

  1) read modCount  
  2) add one  
  3) write it back to modCount  

and another thread could sneak in between those steps and you’d lose updates.  Volatile only guarantees visibility and ordering of reads and writes, not atomic read-modify-write.

You have basically three choices:

1)  If you really need `modCount` to be updated atomically from multiple threads, switch to an `AtomicInteger`.  
2)  If you don’t care about true concurrent modification safety (you’re only using it for a fail-fast iterator in single-threaded usage), drop the `volatile` and ignore the warning.  
3)  Synchronize all mutations on a lock so that `modCount++` is safely protected.

—–––  
Here is a quick example using `AtomicInteger`:  

```java
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

// …  

public class CircularArrayDeQueue<E> implements DeQueue<E> {
  private E[]              array;
  private int              front, rear, size;
  private final AtomicInteger modCount = new AtomicInteger(0);
  private static final int DEFAULT_CAPACITY = 16;

  @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
  public CircularArrayDeQueue() {
    array = (E[]) new Object[DEFAULT_CAPACITY];
  }

  public void pushFirst(E elem) {
    expandIfNeeded();
    front = (front - 1 + array.length) % array.length;
    array[front] = elem;
    size++;
    modCount.incrementAndGet();
  }

  public void pushLast(E elem) {
    expandIfNeeded();
    array[rear] = elem;
    rear = (rear + 1) % array.length;
    size++;
    modCount.incrementAndGet();
  }

  public E popFirst() {
    if (isEmpty()) throw new NoSuchElementException();
    E elem = array[front];
    array[front] = null;
    front = (front + 1) % array.length;
    size--;
    modCount.incrementAndGet();
    shrinkIfNeeded();
    return elem;
  }

  public E popLast() {
    if (isEmpty()) throw new NoSuchElementException();
    rear = (rear - 1 + array.length) % array.length;
    E elem = array[rear];
    array[rear] = null;
    size--;
    modCount.incrementAndGet();
    shrinkIfNeeded();
    return elem;
  }

  public void clear() {
    Arrays.fill(array, null);
    front = rear = size = 0;
    modCount.incrementAndGet();
  }

  // … expandIfNeeded(), shrinkIfNeeded(), resize() all do modCount.incrementAndGet()

  public Iterator<E> iterator() {
    return new DeQueueIterator(front, 1);
  }

  public Iterator<E> descendingIterator() {
    return new DeQueueIterator((rear - 1 + array.length) % array.length, -1);
  }

  private class DeQueueIterator implements Iterator<E> {
    private int           current;
    private final int     step;
    private final int     expectedModCount;

    DeQueueIterator(int start, int step) {
      this.current          = start;
      this.step             = step;
      this.expectedModCount = modCount.get();
    }

    public boolean hasNext() {
      return current != rear;
    }

    public E next() {
      if (modCount.get() != expectedModCount) {
        throw new ConcurrentModificationException();
      }
      E item = array[current];
      current = (current + step + array.length) % array.length;
      return item;
    }
  }
}
```

By using `AtomicInteger.incrementAndGet()` you guarantee that no two threads can lose each other’s increments.  

If you don’t actually intend the queue to be thread-safe, you can also simply make `modCount` a plain `int` (remove `volatile`) and ignore the warning—fail-fast iterators are allowed to be “best effort” in single-threaded use.

Here is a small self‐contained example that shows how to build a compound HDF5 type for

```cpp
struct Data {
    double v[3];
    int    n;
};
```

by hand with HighFive.  The trick is that you must first build an “array‐of‐3 doubles” type for the `v` member, then add that plus the `int` member to a `CompoundType`.

```cpp
#include <highfive/H5File.hpp>
using namespace HighFive;

struct Data {
    double v[3];
    int    n;
};

int main() {
    // 1) Create a new file (truncate if it exists)
    File file("compound_array_example.h5", File::Truncate);

    // 2) Build an ArrayType<double> of length 3
    //    (this is the type of the member v[3])
    DataType array3_dbl = AtomicType<double>().   // base‐type = double
                            createArray({3});      // dimensions = {3}

    // 3) Build the compound type for our struct
    //    We give it the total size of Data, and then add member‐fields
    CompoundType compType(sizeof(Data));
    compType.addMember("v", offsetof(Data, v), array3_dbl);
    compType.addMember("n", offsetof(Data, n), AtomicType<int>());

    // 4) Create a 1D dataset of 10 elements of our compound type
    DataSpace ds = DataSpace({10});
    DataSet dataset = file.createDataSet("mydata", ds, compType);

    // 5) Fill a little std::vector<Data> and write it
    std::vector<Data> w(10);
    for (size_t i = 0; i < w.size(); ++i) {
        w[i].v[0] = i + 0.1;
        w[i].v[1] = i + 0.2;
        w[i].v[2] = i + 0.3;
        w[i].n    = int(i);
    }
    dataset.write(w);

    return 0;
}
```

If you later want to read it back, you simply do:

```cpp
std::vector<Data> r(10);
dataset.read(r);
// r now contains your array+int structs
```

---

Alternative:  If you can change your struct to use `std::array<double,3>` instead of a raw C‐array, you get automatic support from HighFive’s `DataSpace::From` and `createDataType<T>()`, e.g.

```cpp
struct Data2 {
    std::array<double,3> v;
    int                  n;
};

// ...

auto dtype = file.createDataType<Data2>();
auto dataset = file.createDataSet("foo", DataSpace({10}), dtype);
//  DataSpace::From(w) would also work
dataset.write(w2);
```

but that only works if you are free to switch to `std::array`.  If you must keep `double v[3]`, use the manual `CompoundType` construction shown above.

On Linux the kernel simply doesn’t map the “low” megabytes (0x0–0xBFFFFFFF on a 32-bit build, or 0x0–0x0000800000000000 on amd64) into its address space – by design user‐space lives down there and the kernel lives up at PAGE_OFFSET (0xC0000000 on 32-bit, 0xffff880000000000 or 0xffffffff80000000 on 64-bit).  So if you ask for virtual address 0x0010_0000 in your module, you’ll get a page fault because nothing has been wired up down there.

If what you really want is:

  1. a chunk of kernel memory you can copy your shellcode into, and  
  2. that chunk must be executable  

then you have basically two sane choices in a kernel module:

  •  Allocate it via the kernel’s module allocator (vmalloc‐exec area):  
     – module_alloc(size) will give you an executable, page‐aligned region you can copy your code into.  
     – Example:  
       ```c
       #include <linux/moduleloader.h>  /* for module_alloc */
       #include <linux/set_memory.h>    /* for set_memory_x */

       void *code = module_alloc(shellcode_size);
       if (!code) {
         pr_err("module_alloc failed\n");
         return -ENOMEM;
       }
       memcpy(code, shellcode, shellcode_size);
       /* module_alloc normally gives you an RX mapping on x86 kernels ≥ 4.4.
          if you get only RW, promote it to RWE or RX: */
       set_memory_x((unsigned long)code, (shellcode_size+PAGE_SIZE-1)>>PAGE_SHIFT);
       /* now you can call your shellcode as a function pointer */
       ((void(*)(void))code)();
       ```

  •  If you’re really trying to map some *physical* address (say you have a device or
     reserved RAM at phys=0x0010_0000), then use ioremap/ioremap_cache to create a
     kernel‐virtual mapping of it:  
     ```c
     #include <linux/io.h>

     phys_addr_t phys = 0x00100000;
     size_t len     = 0x1000;
     void *vaddr    = ioremap_cache(phys, len);
     if (!vaddr) {
       pr_err("ioremap_cache failed\n");
       return -EIO;
     }
     memcpy(vaddr, shellcode, shellcode_size);
     set_memory_x((unsigned long)vaddr, (len+PAGE_SIZE-1)>>PAGE_SHIFT);
     ((void(*)(void))vaddr)();
     ```

You *cannot* simply pick a kernel‐virtual address in the low megabytes unless you go hack on the architecture’s page‐table setup in arch/… – which you absolutely should not do from a loadable module.  Always allocate or ioremap into the portions of the address space that the kernel already controls.

Here’s a step‐by‐step “baby grep” in Rust. We’ll build a CLI that takes two arguments—a literal substring and a file path—and prints every line in the file that contains that substring.

1) Create a new project  
```bash
$ cargo new babygrep
$ cd babygrep
```

2) Edit src/main.rs:  
```rust
use std::env;
use std::fs;
use std::process;

fn main() {
    // 1. Collect the args into a Vec<String>
    let args: Vec<String> = env::args().collect();

    // 2. Check we have exactly two arguments after the binary name
    if args.len() != 3 {
        eprintln!("Usage: {} <pattern> <file_path>", args[0]);
        process::exit(1);
    }

    let pattern = &args[1];
    let filename = &args[2];

    // 3. Read the file contents (panics on error)
    let contents = fs::read_to_string(filename).unwrap_or_else(|err| {
        eprintln!("Error reading {}: {}", filename, err);
        process::exit(1);
    });

    // 4. Iterate over lines and print those that contain the pattern
    for (idx, line) in contents.lines().enumerate() {
        if line.contains(pattern) {
            println!("{}: {}", idx + 1, line);
        }
    }
}
```

3) Build and run it  
```bash
$ cargo build
$ cargo run -- foo path/to/file.txt
```
This will print every line of `file.txt` that contains the literal substring `foo`, prefixed by its line number.

–––

Next steps / improvements you might consider:  
• Use `BufReader` and `lines()` for streaming large files instead of reading the whole file at once.  
• Swap `unwrap_or_else` + `process::exit` for a `main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>>` and the `?` operator.  
• Add a crate like `clap` or `structopt` for more ergonomic argument parsing (flags, multiple files, case‐insensitive, etc.).  
• Write unit tests around your searching logic by moving it into its own function.  

But the code above is all you need to get started with a minimal substring‐matching CLI in Rust.

The short story is that nothing mystical is wrong with the JVM – you are simply calling Future.get() (or an equivalent) on a task that you (or some library you’re using) have already cancelled.  Java will throw a CancellationException anytime you try to retrieve the result of a cancelled task.

Where it’s happening in your trace:  
  · X.0i9.get(…)  
  · java.util.concurrent.FutureTask.get()  

How to fix it:

  1. Find out who is calling cancel(...)  
     – Somewhere in your code (or in a library like an AsyncTask, Glide, Volley, etc.) you are invoking  
          someFuture.cancel(true)  
       or  
          asyncTask.cancel(true).  
     – That causes the task to go into a “cancelled” state. Any subsequent .get() on the underlying FutureTask will throw CancellationException.

  2. Decide what you really want to do:  
     a) If you never meant to cancel the task, simply remove the cancel(...) call.  
     b) If cancellation is intentional and you still want to call get(), wrap it in a try/catch or guard it with isCancelled():  

          if (!someFuture.isCancelled()) {
            try {
              Object result = someFuture.get();
              // … use result …
            } catch (CancellationException ce) {
              // task was cancelled – handle gracefully
            } catch (ExecutionException ee) {
              // task threw an exception – handle that
            } catch (InterruptedException ie) {
              // current thread was interrupted while waiting
              Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
          } else {
            // skip the get() – task was already cancelled
          }

     c) If you’re using an API that internally cancels tasks for you (e.g. Android’s AsyncTask onActivityDestroyed, or an HTTP client that times out requests), switch to its callback/listener model instead of calling get() yourself.

  3. If you need a timeout instead of a hard cancel, you can do:  

        try {
          Object result = someFuture.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        } catch (TimeoutException te) {
          // didn’t complete in time
          someFuture.cancel(true);
        }  


In 99% of cases the fix is simply “don’t call get() on a Future you’ve already cancelled” or “catch the CancellationException if cancellation is a valid outcome.”

Here’s one way to get a “global” mock in your setup file and then override it on a per-test basis. The trick is:

1. In your setupFilesAfterEnv you call jest.mock(...) to install your default/mock.  
2. In the individual test you either:  
   - call a new top-level jest.mock(...) (hoisted above your imports), or  
   - call jest.unmock(...) + jest.doMock(...) (or jest.resetModules) before you require the code under test.

—————————————————————————————————————————————  
1) Jest config (jest.config.js)  
```js
module.exports = {
  // …
  setupFilesAfterEnv: [ "<rootDir>/jest.setup.js" ],
  // …
}
```

2) Global default mock (jest.setup.js)  
```js
// this runs before each test file
jest.mock("./lib/api", () => {
  // default implementation for all tests
  return {
    fetchData: jest.fn(() => Promise.resolve({ data: "DEFAULT" }))
  };
});
```

Now every time your code under test does

   import { fetchData } from "./lib/api"

it gets that default mock.

—————————————————————————————————————————————  
3) Override in a single test file  

Option A) top-level jest.mock (hoisted)  
```js
// myComponent.test.js

// override the mock from jest.setup.js
jest.mock("../lib/api", () => ({
  fetchData: jest.fn(() => Promise.resolve({ data: "OVERRIDDEN" }))
}));

import { fetchData } from "../lib/api";
import { renderComponent } from "../myComponent";

test("uses the OVERRIDDEN mock", async () => {
  const result = await fetchData();
  expect(result.data).toBe("OVERRIDDEN");

  // you can also verify your component under test
  const output = await renderComponent();
  expect(output).toMatch("OVERRIDDEN");
});
```
Because jest.mock is hoisted, this mock replaces the one from the setup file for this test.

Option B) jest.unmock + jest.doMock + resetModules  
```js
// myOtherComponent.test.js

beforeEach(() => {
  // clear out all modules from the require cache so that when
  // we re-require them after mocking, they pick up the new mock
  jest.resetModules();

  // remove the setupFile mock
  jest.unmock("../lib/api");

  // install our custom one
  jest.doMock("../lib/api", () => ({
    fetchData: jest.fn(() => Promise.resolve({ data: "MY_CUSTOM" }))
  }));
});

test("uses MY_CUSTOM mock", async () => {
  // now require() will pick up the new mock
  const { fetchData } = require("../lib/api");
  const output = await fetchData();
  expect(output.data).toBe("MY_CUSTOM");
});
```

Notes on both approaches:
- Option A only works if you never import `"../lib/api"` before the top-level `jest.mock` call in your test file.  
- Option B works even if your code under test imported `"../lib/api"` at the top, because you `resetModules()` and then `require()` after you’ve re-mocked.  

Either one will let you ship a default mock in your `setupFilesAfterEnv` and still override per-test.

