In ES Modules you really only have two “built-in” ways of getting a Util-style namespace:

  1. import everything under a namespace when you consume the module  
     ```js
     // util.js
     export function doSomething() { … }
     export function doSomethingElse() { … }

     // app.js
     import * as Util from './util.js'
     Util.doSomething()
     Util.doSomethingElse()
     ```

  2. explicitly bundle your functions into one default export
     ```js
     // util.js
     export function doSomething() { … }
     export function doSomethingElse() { … }

     // create an object that points to your functions
     const Util = { doSomething, doSomethingElse }

     // named-exports are optional at this point:
     export { doSomething, doSomethingElse }

     // default export:
     export default Util
     ```
     Then in your consumer:
     ```js
     import Util from './util.js'
     Util.doSomething()
     Util.doSomethingElse()
     ```

If you really hate having to list the functions twice, you can even “self-import” and re-export the namespace as the default (modern browsers/node will handle the circular import gracefully):

```js
// util.js
export function doSomething() { … }
export function doSomethingElse() { … }

// grab *all* named exports into an object
import * as _all from './util.js'

// re-export that object as your default
export default _all
```

Now anybody can do

```js
import Util from './util.js'
Util.doSomething()
Util.doSomethingElse()
```

Choose whichever pattern feels clearest for your project.

Node’s old “classic” streams are really pull‐based under the hood (they only call your _read() when the consumer asks for data), but most people use them in “flowing” (push) mode via pipe() or on('data').  If you really want a pull‐only API there are a few options:

1) Override _read() on a custom Readable  
----------------------------------------  
When the user calls .read() (or pipe/resume() happens) Node will invoke your _read() to get more data.  No one ever asked for data → your _read() never runs.

```js
const { Readable } = require('stream');

class PullOnlyStream extends Readable {
  constructor(opts) {
    super(opts);
    this._counter = 0;
  }

  // This method is invoked *only* when the user
  // asks for data (via .read() or pipe())
  _read(size) {
    if (this._counter >= 5) {
      // no more data
      return this.push(null);
    }
    // produce a chunk:
    const chunk = Buffer.from(String(this._counter++));
    // push it downstream
    this.push(chunk);
  }
}

const s = new PullOnlyStream();

// Manual pull:
s.on('readable', () => {
  let chunk;
  // each s.read() here invokes your _read() if buffer is empty
  while ((chunk = s.read()) !== null) {
    console.log('got:', chunk.toString());
  }
});

// Or still pipe it if you like:
// s.pipe(process.stdout);
```

2) Use Readable.from() with an async generator  
------------------------------------------------  
Under the covers Readable.from() will pull new values from your async iterator only when the consumer needs them:

```js
const { Readable } = require('stream');

async function* gen() {
  for (let i=0; i<5; i++) {
    // fake async work
    await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r,100));
    yield Buffer.from(String(i));
  }
}

const pullStream = Readable.from(gen());

// same “pull” behavior:
pullStream.on('readable', () => {
  let c;
  while ((c = pullStream.read()) !== null) {
    console.log('> ', c.toString());
  }
});
```

3) Use the new WHATWG/Web-Streams API  
--------------------------------------  
Node 16+ exposes a built-in Web-Streams implementation.  A ReadableStream here is *literally* pull‐based:

```js
import { ReadableStream } from 'stream/web';

const pullStream = new ReadableStream({
  start(controller) {
    this._i = 0;
  },
  pull(controller) {
    if (this._i >= 5) {
      controller.close();
      return;
    }
    controller.enqueue(String(this._i++));
  }
});

// To consume, you get a reader:
const reader = pullStream.getReader();
(async () => {
  let res;
  while (!(res = await reader.read()).done) {
    console.log('pulled:', res.value);
  }
})();
```

4) Use a community “pull‐stream” module  
----------------------------------------  
If you’d rather go fully functional you can grab https://github.com/pull-stream/pull-stream and compose sources, sinks and throughs in the pull‐style that Dominic Tarr defined.

---

In most cases simply overriding `_read()` or using `Readable.from(async function*...)` gives you exactly the “only fetch when asked” semantics you’re after.

In JavaScript only Arrays (and a few array‐like things) have a built-in .map() method. Plain Objects don’t. So you have two choices:

1) Turn your data into an Array  
2) Use one of the Object.* helpers to map its keys or values

## 1) If you really meant an array:
```js
// instead of an object, make x an array:
let x = [1,2,3];     // now x.map will exist
let y = x.map(z => z + 1);
console.log(y);      // [2,3,4]
```

## 2) If you really want to transform the values in an Object:
a) Using Object.values():
```js
let x = { one: 1, two: 2, three: 3 };

// this gives you an array of values [1,2,3], you map those,
// but if you want to put them back into an object you have to rebuild it:
let valuesPlusOne = Object.values(x).map(v => v + 1);
console.log(valuesPlusOne);    // [2,3,4]

// if you want a new object with the same keys:
let entriesPlusOne = Object.entries(x)
  .map(([k,v]) => [k, v + 1]);     // [ ['one',2], ['two',3], ... ]
let newObj = Object.fromEntries(entriesPlusOne);
console.log(newObj);    // { one: 2, two: 3, three: 4 }
```

b) Using Object.keys():
```js
let x = { one: 1, two: 2 };

let result = Object.keys(x).map(key => {
  return { [key]: x[key] + 1 };
});
console.log(result);
// [ { one: 2 }, { two: 3 } ]
```

c) Using a for-in loop (old-school but clear):
```js
let x = { one: 1, two: 2 };
let out = {};
for (let k in x) {
  if (x.hasOwnProperty(k)) {
    out[k] = x[k] + 1;
  }
}
console.log(out);  // { one: 2, two: 3 }
```

Pick whichever approach matches your goal:
– if you actually need array semantics, use an Array.  
– if you need to transform the values of an object, use Object.keys/Object.values/Object.entries (plus Object.fromEntries if you need to get back to an Object).

Below is a minimal example of a separable Gaussian‐blur in Metal.  We do it in two passes (horizontal + vertical), which is far cheaper than doing a full 2D convolution in one kernel.

Save this as GaussianBlur.metal:

```metal
#include <metal_stdlib>
using namespace metal;

// Uniforms that we pass in from the CPU
struct BlurUniforms {
    uint   width;   // texture width
    uint   height;  // texture height
    uint   radius;  // blur radius in pixels
    float  sigma;   // standard deviation of the gaussian
};

// First pass: horizontal blur
kernel void gaussianBlurHorizontal(
    texture2d<float, access::read>   inTex   [[ texture(0) ]],
    texture2d<float, access::write>  outTex  [[ texture(1) ]],
    constant BlurUniforms&           uni     [[ buffer(0) ]],
    uint2                            gid     [[ thread_position_in_grid ]])
{
    // bounds check
    if (gid.x >= uni.width || gid.y >= uni.height) return;

    float4  sum        = float4(0.0);
    float   weightSum  = 0.0;
    float   twoSigmaSq = 2.0 * uni.sigma * uni.sigma;
    float   norm       = 1.0 / (sqrt(2.0 * M_PI) * uni.sigma);

    // convolve horizontally
    for (int i = -int(uni.radius); i <= int(uni.radius); i++) {
        int sampleX = clamp(int(gid.x) + i, 0, int(uni.width) - 1);
        float w = norm * exp(-float(i*i) / twoSigmaSq);
        sum       += w * inTex.read(uint2(sampleX, gid.y));
        weightSum += w;
    }
    outTex.write(sum / weightSum, gid);
}

// Second pass: vertical blur
kernel void gaussianBlurVertical(
    texture2d<float, access::read>   inTex   [[ texture(0) ]],
    texture2d<float, access::write>  outTex  [[ texture(1) ]],
    constant BlurUniforms&           uni     [[ buffer(0) ]],
    uint2                            gid     [[ thread_position_in_grid ]])
{
    if (gid.x >= uni.width || gid.y >= uni.height) return;

    float4  sum        = float4(0.0);
    float   weightSum  = 0.0;
    float   twoSigmaSq = 2.0 * uni.sigma * uni.sigma;
    float   norm       = 1.0 / (sqrt(2.0 * M_PI) * uni.sigma);

    // convolve vertically
    for (int i = -int(uni.radius); i <= int(uni.radius); i++) {
        int sampleY = clamp(int(gid.y) + i, 0, int(uni.height) - 1);
        float w = norm * exp(-float(i*i) / twoSigmaSq);
        sum       += w * inTex.read(uint2(gid.x, sampleY));
        weightSum += w;
    }
    outTex.write(sum / weightSum, gid);
}
```

Usage outline on the CPU (Swift):

```swift
// 1. Create your device, commandQueue, defaultLibrary...
let library = device.makeDefaultLibrary()!
let horzPSO = try! device.makeComputePipelineState(function: library.makeFunction(name: "gaussianBlurHorizontal")!)
let vertPSO = try! device.makeComputePipelineState(function: library.makeFunction(name: "gaussianBlurVertical")!)

// 2. Create two intermediate textures of the same size as your source.
//    tex0 = original, tex1 = temp, tex2 = final

// 3. Fill your BlurUniforms
var uniforms = BlurUniforms(
    width:  UInt(srcTex.width),
    height: UInt(srcTex.height),
    radius: 10,
    sigma:  4.0
)

// 4. Encode horizontal pass
let cmdBuf = commandQueue.makeCommandBuffer()!
let encoder = cmdBuf.makeComputeCommandEncoder()!
encoder.setComputePipelineState(horzPSO)
encoder.setTexture(srcTex, index: 0)
encoder.setTexture(tmpTex, index: 1)
encoder.setBytes(&uniforms, length: MemoryLayout<BlurUniforms>.stride, index: 0)

let w = horzPSO.threadExecutionWidth
let h = horzPSO.maxTotalThreadsPerThreadgroup / w
let tg = MTLSize(width: w, height: h, depth: 1)
let grid = MTLSize(width: srcTex.width, height: srcTex.height, depth: 1)
encoder.dispatchThreads(grid, threadsPerThreadgroup: tg)
encoder.endEncoding()

// 5. Encode vertical pass (reading tmpTex, writing outTex)
let encoder2 = cmdBuf.makeComputeCommandEncoder()!
encoder2.setComputePipelineState(vertPSO)
encoder2.setTexture(tmpTex, index: 0)
encoder2.setTexture(outTex, index: 1)
encoder2.setBytes(&uniforms, length: MemoryLayout<BlurUniforms>.stride, index: 0)
encoder2.dispatchThreads(grid, threadsPerThreadgroup: tg)
encoder2.endEncoding()

cmdBuf.commit()
cmdBuf.waitUntilCompleted()
// now outTex holds the blurred image
```

Notes:

1. We compute the Gaussian weight on‐the‐fly with `exp(–x²/2σ²)` and renormalize so the weights sum to 1.
2. Separable convolution (two 1D passes) reduces work from O(r²) per pixel to O(r).
3. Tweak `radius` and `sigma` to control the blur strength. A common rule of thumb is `radius ≃ 3⋅sigma`.

