In PHP8.2 itself there is no “built-in” coroutine‐pool, but if you use Swoole’s coroutine feature (or Amp) you can easily throttle yourself to N concurrent jobs.  Two of the most common patterns are:

 1. a fixed‐size Channel as a semaphore  
 2. the Swoole\Coroutine\Semaphore class  

---  
1) Using Swoole\Coroutine\Channel as a mutex/semaphore  

```php
<?php
use Swoole\Coroutine;
use Swoole\Coroutine\Channel;

Coroutine\run(function() {
    // max 5 coroutines at a time
    $sem = new Channel(5);

    $jobs = [
        'http://example.com/1',
        'http://example.com/2',
        // … your URLs or tasks
    ];

    foreach ($jobs as $url) {
        // push a token into the channel
        // when the channel has 5 tokens in it, push() will block
        $sem->push(true);

        Coroutine::create(function() use ($url, $sem) {
            // do your I/O bound or CPU bound work
            $cli = new Swoole\Coroutine\Http\Client('example.com', 80);
            $cli->set(['timeout' => 3]);
            $cli->get('/');

            echo "Fetched {$url} in coroutine #", Coroutine::getCid(), "\n";

            // release the slot
            $sem->pop();
        });
    }
});
```

Explanation:

- We create a `Channel(5)` which can hold 5 items.  
- Before starting each job we do `$sem->push(true)`. If there are already 5 items in the channel, `push()` will block until one is popped.  
- When the job is done we call `$sem->pop()` to free up a slot.  

This guarantees you’ll never have more than 5 coroutines actively working your tasks.

---  
2) Using Swoole\Coroutine\Semaphore  

```php
<?php
use Swoole\Coroutine;
use Swoole\Coroutine\Semaphore;

Coroutine\run(function() {
    $max = 5;
    $sem = new Semaphore($max);

    $jobs = [
        'job1',
        'job2',
        'job3',
        // …
    ];

    foreach ($jobs as $job) {
        Coroutine::create(function() use ($sem, $job) {
            // wait() will block if we've already acquired the semaphore max times
            $sem->wait();
            try {
                // your job processing
                process_job($job);
                echo "Done {$job} in coro #", Coroutine::getCid(), "\n";
            } finally {
                // always release in a finally
                $sem->post();
            }
        });
    }
});

function process_job(string $job): void
{
    // simulate work
    Co::sleep(mt_rand(1, 3));
}
```

Explanation:

- `Semaphore(5)` gives you a “counter” initially set to 5.  
- `wait()` decrements it; if it’s already zero, `wait()` blocks.  
- When you’re done with the slot, you call `post()` to increment it again.

---

3) If you’re using Amp instead of Swoole  

Amp provides `LocalSemaphore` (or `Semaphore` in Amp v3).  Example:

```php
<?php
use Amp\Loop;
use Amp\Sync\LocalSemaphore;
use function Amp\async;

Loop::run(function() {
    $jobs = ['a','b','c','d','e','f','g'];
    $sem  = new LocalSemaphore(5);

    foreach ($jobs as $job) {
        async(function() use ($sem, $job) {
            // withLock() acquires and releases for you
            yield $sem->withLock(function() use ($job) {
                // your async work here
                echo "Processing {$job}\n";
                // simulate I/O
                yield Amp\delay(500);
            });
        });
    }
});
```

All three techniques achieve the same goal: no more than N coroutines are actively working at any one time. Pick the one that matches your coroutine library.

Here’s two safe ways to build your XPath expression so that un‐trusted values for “user” and “pass” can’t break out and inject arbitrary XPath:

1) Using OWASP ESAPI’s XPath encoder  
------------------------------------------------  
```java
import javax.xml.xpath.*;
import org.owasp.esapi.ESAPI;
import org.owasp.esapi.Encoder;
import org.w3c.dom.Document;
import org.w3c.dom.NodeList;

// ...

Encoder encoder = ESAPI.encoder();

// escape user inputs for XPath
String safeUser = encoder.encodeForXPath(user);
String safePass = encoder.encodeForXPath(pass);

// now build the expression
String expression = "/users/user"
  + "[@name='" + safeUser + "'"
  + " and @pass='" + safePass + "']";

// compile & evaluate
XPath xpath = XPathFactory.newInstance().newXPath();
XPathExpression expr = xpath.compile(expression);
NodeList nodes = (NodeList)expr.evaluate(doc, XPathConstants.NODESET);
```

ESAPI’s encodeForXPath will properly neutralize quotes, brackets, etc., preventing injections.

2) Rolling‐your‐own “escape” that uses XPath’s concat(…) trick  
------------------------------------------------  
If you don’t want an external library, you can escape single quotes by transforming any value containing `'` into an XPath concat(), e.g.:

```java
/**
 * Escape a raw string for use inside an XPath literal.
 * If it contains no single‐quotes, we wrap in '...'.
 * Otherwise we split on ' and join pieces with , "'", 
 * producing a concat('foo', "'", 'bar') call.
 */
public static String escapeForXpath(String s) {
  if (s.indexOf('\'') < 0) {
    // no single‐quotes → safe to wrap
    return "'" + s + "'";
  }
  String[] parts = s.split("'");
  StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("concat(");
  for (int i = 0; i < parts.length; i++) {
    sb.append("'").append(parts[i]).append("'");
    if (i < parts.length - 1) {
      sb.append(", \"'\", ");
    }
  }
  sb.append(")");
  return sb.toString();
}

// …

// apply escaping
String expr = "/users/user"
  + "[@name=" + escapeForXpath(user)
  + " and @pass=" + escapeForXpath(pass)
  + "]";

XPath xpath = XPathFactory.newInstance().newXPath();
XPathExpression xexp = xpath.compile(expr);
NodeList list = (NodeList)xexp.evaluate(doc, XPathConstants.NODESET);
```

How it works:  
- If `user` is `"O'Brian"`, `escapeForXpath` will turn it into  
  `concat('O', "'", 'Brian')`, which is a legal XPath string literal.  
- Any attempt to inject `'] | //admin[...` will get neutralized because stray quotes no longer terminate your literal.

Either of these approaches prevents malicious data from breaking out of the quoted predicate and performing an injection.

Below is a self‐contained ES6 example of the Strategy Pattern in a “fun” banking app scenario.  In our “NinjaVault Bank,” customers have different account levels (Rookie, Shadow, Master) and each level uses a different transaction‐fee strategy when they withdraw money.

```javascript
// === 1) Define the Strategy Interface ===
// In JS we don’t strictly enforce interfaces, but by convention each strategy
// implements a calculateFee(amount) method.

class FeeStrategy {
  calculateFee(amount) {
    throw new Error("This method should be overridden!");
  }
}

// === 2) Concrete Strategies ===

class RookieFeeStrategy extends FeeStrategy {
  // Rookie ninjas pay a flat 5% fee
  calculateFee(amount) {
    return amount * 0.05;
  }
}

class ShadowFeeStrategy extends FeeStrategy {
  // Shadow ninjas pay a small 2% fee + $1
  calculateFee(amount) {
    return amount * 0.02 + 1;
  }
}

class MasterFeeStrategy extends FeeStrategy {
  // Master ninjas are VIPs: no fee
  calculateFee(amount) {
    return 0;
  }
}

// === 3) The Context ===

class BankAccount {
  constructor(ownerName, startingBalance = 0, feeStrategy) {
    this.ownerName = ownerName;
    this.balance = startingBalance;
    // inject a fee strategy:
    this.feeStrategy = feeStrategy;
  }

  setFeeStrategy(newStrategy) {
    console.log(`${this.ownerName} is changing strategy to ${newStrategy.constructor.name}`);
    this.feeStrategy = newStrategy;
  }

  deposit(amount) {
    if (amount <= 0) {
      console.log("Deposit amount must be positive.");
      return;
    }
    this.balance += amount;
    console.log(`${this.ownerName} deposited $${amount.toFixed(2)}. New balance: $${this.balance.toFixed(2)}`);
  }

  withdraw(amount) {
    if (amount <= 0) {
      console.log("Withdraw amount must be positive.");
      return;
    }
    const fee = this.feeStrategy.calculateFee(amount);
    const total = amount + fee;
    if (total > this.balance) {
      console.log(`Insufficient funds. You need $${total.toFixed(2)}, but only have $${this.balance.toFixed(2)}.`);
      return;
    }
    this.balance -= total;
    console.log(
      `${this.ownerName} withdrew $${amount.toFixed(2)} ` +
      `(fee $${fee.toFixed(2)}). Balance left: $${this.balance.toFixed(2)}`
    );
  }

  getBalance() {
    return this.balance;
  }
}

// === 4) Putting it all together ===

// Create some strategy instances
const rookieStrategy  = new RookieFeeStrategy();
const shadowStrategy  = new ShadowFeeStrategy();
const masterStrategy  = new MasterFeeStrategy();

// A rookie ninja opens an account
const akiraAccount = new BankAccount("Akira the Rookie", 100, rookieStrategy);
akiraAccount.withdraw(20);
// → Akira the Rookie withdrew $20.00 (fee $1.00). Balance left: $79.00

// Akira levels up to Shadow
akiraAccount.setFeeStrategy(shadowStrategy);
akiraAccount.withdraw(20);
// → Akira the Rookie withdrew $20.00 (fee $1.40). Balance left: $57.60

// Akira masters the art and becomes Master Ninja (no fees!)
akiraAccount.setFeeStrategy(masterStrategy);
akiraAccount.withdraw(20);
// → Akira the Rookie withdrew $20.00 (fee $0.00). Balance left: $37.60

// Deposit some loot
akiraAccount.deposit(50);
// → Akira the Rookie deposited $50.00. New balance: $87.60
```

Explanation of how the Strategy Pattern is used here:

• FeeStrategy “interface”: we define a base class with `calculateFee()` that all concrete strategies override.  
• Concrete strategies (`RookieFeeStrategy`, `ShadowFeeStrategy`, `MasterFeeStrategy`) encapsulate different fee‐calculation algorithms.  
• Context (`BankAccount`) holds a reference to a fee strategy and delegates `calculateFee` to it. You can swap strategies at runtime with `setFeeStrategy(...)` without changing the `BankAccount` code.

This pattern makes it super‐easy to add new account levels or promotional fee schemes in the future—just create another strategy class and inject it!

