Here’s a cleaned-up version that:

1. Uses RTK-Query’s `skip` option so you don’t fire the query if no GUID is provided  
2. Drops the unnecessary `!!!` check in favor of a plain falsy check  
3. Adds a `useMemo` around the mapping so you don’t re-map on every render  
4. Handles a loading state (optional)  

```tsx
import React, { useMemo } from "react"
import FieldDropDown from "lib/hookForm/fieldDropDown"
import type { ICompanyLogo } from "services/api/company/companyTypes"
import { useGetCompanyLogosInfoByLoanGuidQuery } from "services/api/loan/apiLoanQuery"

interface IProps {
  /**
   * Primary key for fetching logos.
   * Falls back to companyGuid if forcePlaceLoanGuid is undefined.
   */
  forcePlaceLoanGuid?: string
  companyGuid?: string
}

export default function LoanLogoDropdown({ forcePlaceLoanGuid, companyGuid }: IProps) {
  // pick whichever GUID is available (or undefined)
  const guid = forcePlaceLoanGuid ?? companyGuid

  // only fire the query if we actually have a GUID
  const { data: logos, isLoading, isError } =
    useGetCompanyLogosInfoByLoanGuidQuery(guid ?? "", {
      skip: !guid,
    })

  // optionally render a spinner / fallback while loading
  if (isLoading) {
    return <div>Loading logos…</div>
  }

  // no data or error => don’t render the dropdown
  if (!logos || isError) {
    return null
  }

  // memoize mapping so it only recalculates when `logos` changes
  const options = useMemo(
    () =>
      logos.map((logo: ICompanyLogo) => ({
        key: logo.portfolioIdentifier,
        value: logo.logoDescription,
      })),
    [logos]
  )

  return (
    <FieldDropDown
      label="Company Logo"
      name="portfolioIdentifier"
      data={options}
      placeholder="Select Logo"
      labelColSize={3}
      inputColSize={9}
    />
  )
}
```

Key improvements:

• Don’t invoke the query at all when you don’t have a GUID (via `skip`)  
• Remove the confusing `if (!!!companyLogos)` check in favor of `if (!logos)`  
• Wrap your map in `useMemo` so it only runs when the data changes  
• Optionally handle loading / error states so the UI doesn’t abruptly pop in and out

In Rust every closure automatically implements at least FnOnce; it will also implement FnMut or Fn only if it doesn’t do anything “consuming” or mutating with its captures:

  • FnOnce: you can call it exactly once (it might take ownership of things).  
  • FnMut: you can call it multiple times, but it might mutate its captures.  
  • Fn: you can call it any number of times without mutation or consuming.

If you get an error like:

  expected a closure that implements the `Fn` trait, but this closure only implements `FnOnce`

it means you’ve written a closure that “takes” ownership of something from its environment (or otherwise mutates/takes things) and you’re trying to pass it to an API that requires a pure Fn (i.e. callable any number of times without consumption).

You have three principal ways to fix it:

1. Change the API to accept FnOnce (or FnMut) rather than Fn  
2. Change your closure so that it only borrows (and doesn’t move) anything from its environment  
3. Wrap your owned data in a shared pointer so that the closure can be Fn

—–––––––––––––––––––––––––––––

1) Change the trait bound

If you own the function that is complaining, you can relax its bound. For example, instead of:

    // currently only Fn closures will fit here:
    fn call_twice<F>(f: F)
      where F: Fn() {
        f();
        f();
    }

you could make it accept FnOnce (if you only need one call):

    fn call_once<F>(f: F)
      where F: FnOnce() {
        f();
    }

Or if you need to call it twice but it only works once, you could consume it by e.g. boxing/replacing:

    fn call_twice_boxed(f: Box<dyn FnOnce()>) {
      // call first time
      f(); 
      // you can't call it a second time, it was FnOnce…
    }

more realistically you’d adjust the logic so you only need one invocation.

—–––––––––––––––––––––––––––––

2) Don’t capture by value

Often the closure “only” needs to borrow something.  If you wrote:

    let s = String::from("hello");
    call_twice(move || {
      println!("{}", s);
    });

the `move` closure takes ownership of `s`, so it only implements FnOnce.  If the function needs an Fn, try removing `move` (and make sure you only borrow `s`):

    let s = String::from("hello");
    // no `move` → closure only borrows `&s`
    call_twice(|| {
      println!("{}", s);
    });

Now your closure doesn’t consume `s`, so it implements Fn.

—–––––––––––––––––––––––––––––

3) Share ownership with Rc/Arc or RefCell

If you really do need a “shared, owned” pointer inside an Fn, wrap it:

    use std::rc::Rc;

    let counter = Rc::new(std::cell::Cell::new(0));
    // clone the Rc so the closure only holds an Rc (a cheap, copyable handle)
    let cc = counter.clone();
    call_twice(move || {
      cc.set(cc.get() + 1);
      println!("count: {}", cc.get());
    });

Here the closure only captures an Rc<Cell<_>> by value; that type implements Clone + Copy-ish borrow rules, so the closure is pure Fn.

—–––––––––––––––––––––––––––––

Typical checklist when you see “only implements FnOnce”:

  • Did you use `move` and take ownership of a non-Copy type?  
  • Are you calling `.into_iter()` or otherwise consuming a captured variable?  
  • Does your closure mutate or take ownership?  

If you can rewrite it to only borrow (or wrap in a shared pointer) it will become Fn. Otherwise you must change the callee to accept FnOnce or FnMut.

Here’s a simple Rust function that takes a month name or abbreviation (case‐insensitive) or a numeric string and returns its corresponding month number (1–12) as an `Option<u8>`. Unrecognized inputs yield `None`.

```rust
/// Convert a month name, abbreviation, or numeric string into its month number (1–12).
/// 
/// Returns:
/// - `Some(1)` for "January", "Jan", "1", "01", etc.
/// - `Some(2)` for "February", "Feb", "2", "02", etc.
/// - …
/// - `None` for any unrecognized input.
pub fn month_to_number(input: &str) -> Option<u8> {
    // Normalize: trim whitespace and lowercase
    let s = input.trim().to_lowercase();
    match s.as_str() {
        // January
        "1" | "01" | "january" | "jan" => Some(1),
        // February
        "2" | "02" | "february" | "feb" => Some(2),
        // March
        "3" | "03" | "march" | "mar" => Some(3),
        // April
        "4" | "04" | "april" | "apr" => Some(4),
        // May
        "5" | "05" | "may"         => Some(5),
        // June
        "6" | "06" | "june" | "jun" => Some(6),
        // July
        "7" | "07" | "july" | "jul" => Some(7),
        // August
        "8" | "08" | "august" | "aug" => Some(8),
        // September
        "9" | "09" | "september" | "sep" | "sept" => Some(9),
        // October
        "10" | "october" | "oct"   => Some(10),
        // November
        "11" | "november" | "nov"  => Some(11),
        // December
        "12" | "december" | "dec"  => Some(12),
        // Catch all
        _ => None,
    }
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::month_to_number;

    #[test]
    fn test_full_names() {
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("January"), Some(1));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("febRUARY"), Some(2));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("March"), Some(3));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("april"), Some(4));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("May"), Some(5));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("jUne"), Some(6));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("July"), Some(7));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("August"), Some(8));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("September"), Some(9));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("October"), Some(10));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("November"), Some(11));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("December"), Some(12));
    }

    #[test]
    fn test_abbreviations() {
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("Jan"), Some(1));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("feb"), Some(2));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("Mar"), Some(3));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("APR"), Some(4));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("may"), Some(5));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("jun"), Some(6));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("Jul"), Some(7));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("aug"), Some(8));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("sept"), Some(9));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("Oct"), Some(10));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("nov"), Some(11));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("DEC"), Some(12));
    }

    #[test]
    fn test_numeric() {
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("1"), Some(1));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("01"), Some(1));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("12"), Some(12));
        assert_eq!(month_to_number(" 07 "), Some(7)); // trimming works
    }

    #[test]
    fn test_invalid() {
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("Foo"), None);
        assert_eq!(month_to_number("13"), None);
        assert_eq!(month_to_number(""), None);
    }
}
```

Explanation  
1. We normalize the input by trimming whitespace and lowercasing.  
2. We match against all full month names, common three‐letter abbreviations, and numeric strings (with or without leading zero).  
3. We return `Some(1..=12)` on a match, or `None` otherwise.  

You can drop the numeric branches if you only want name‐to‐number conversion. If you need more extensibility (e.g. localization), consider building a `HashMap<&'static str, u8>` at startup or using a crate like `strum` to auto‐derive parsing for a `Month` enum.

